  "rewrites": {
   "6": {
    "original": "The paper's long author list of researchers from Belgium, Bulgaria, India, Japan, and the UK signals the increasing interest from scientists in studying the genetic underpinnings of the yeasts used industrially, say the authors.",
    "rewrite": "The lengthy list of authors from Belgium, Bulgaria, India, Japan, and the UK in the paper reflects growing scientific interest in examining the genetic basis of industrially used yeasts, according to the authors."
   },
   "7": {
    "original": "And although Gregor Mendel's pea-plant experiments were central to the development of genetic research, his scientific discoveries don't apply to yeast, which is diploid but can form tetraploid cells.",
    "rewrite": "Despite the pivotal role of Gregor Mendel's pea-plant experiments in the development of genetic research, his scientific discoveries do not extend to yeast, which has a diploid structure and can produce tetraploid cells."
   },
   "2": {
    "original": "The researchers speculate that early beer brewers could have increased their success by approaching stronger alcoholic beverages by crossing different yeast strains.",
    "rewrite": "The researchers hypothesize that early beer producers might have boosted their outcomes by producing stronger alcohol contents through crossbreeding various yeast strains."
   },
   "1": {
    "original": "Keeping a separate female strain to be sexed with also promotes CGT, which increases fermentation efficiency, but not all beer strains carry this gene.",
    "rewrite": "Maintaining a distinct female line for mating purposes boosts CGT, enhancing fermentation efficiency, although not every yeast strain possesses this gene."
   },
   "8": {
    "original": "Tetraploids may also have led to strains that are missing an allele, sometimes for a crucial gene that differs from one parental strain to the next.",
    "rewrite": "Strains that lack an allele, including those for essential genes, might also have arisen from tetraploids, varying from one parental strain to another."
   },
   "12": {
    "original": "The discovery of nitrogen, for example, has revolutionized beer-making, but some old brewing methods remain.",
    "rewrite": "For instance, the identification of nitrogen has transformed beer production, yet certain traditional brewing techniques are still used."
   },
   "11": {
    "original": "Changes in beer-drinking culture have also shaped how yeast has evolved over the last centuries.",
    "rewrite": "Changes in beer-drinking habits have also guided the evolution of yeast over the past centuries."
   }
  },
  "final_span": "ability, which gives them increased stress tolerance. Maintaining a distinct female line for mating purposes boosts CGT, enhancing fermentation efficiency, although not every yeast strain possesses this gene. The researchers hypothesize that early beer producers might have boosted their outcomes by producing stronger alcohol contents through crossbreeding various yeast strains.\n\"This research shows that agriculture may go far back,\" says Verstrepen. \"We don't know exactly how many years ago people started to manipulate strains, but this research shows that it goes far back. It can be categorized as pre-writing revolution.\"\nThe lengthy list of authors from Belgium, Bulgaria, India, Japan, and the UK in the paper reflects growing scientific interest in examining the genetic basis of industrially used yeasts, according to the authors.\nDespite the pivotal role of Gregor Mendel's pea-plant experiments in the development of genetic research, his scientific discoveries do not extend to yeast, which has a diploid structure and can produce tetraploid cells. Strains that lack an allele, including those for essential genes, might also have arisen from tetraploids, varying from one parental strain to another.\n\"You can even blame it on Mendel,\" says Verstrepen. \"It's so easy to pick out the good alleles in a parent gene, but humans' smart choice to breed multiple strains together makes it more likely to select for bad alleles.\"\nChanges in beer-drinking habits have also guided the evolution of yeast over the past centuries. For instance, the identification of nitrogen has transformed beer production, yet certain traditional brewing techniques are still used. \"The different beers in Belgium have become a holy cow,\" says Verstrepen. \"And that's a good thing, because today we have no idea of how you make real spontaneous beer, and it's gone. The art of beer-making is basically gone.\"\n\"This plurality, not just of different yeasts but of different ways of getting genetic variation, and the extreme differences in beer styles surrounding that, are very much dictated by the social and cultural climate surrounding beer,\" says",
  "break_point": {
   "step": 23,
   "sentences_rewritten": 7,
   "queries": 1116
  }
 },
